# Control properties are split per service so ccci_mdinit cannot control
# unrelated vendor daemons.
#
# ctl_mtk_ccci_rpcd_prop and ctl_mtk_muxreport_prop have NO setter, and that is
# the point rather than an omission: an unlabelled ctl.vendor.* falls through
# plat_property_contexts' `ctl.` prefix to ctl_default_prop, which
# private/system_app.te:62, private/system_server.te:595, private/usbd.te:12
# and public/wificond.te:12 can all set. Deleting these two lines would
# therefore let any system_app start and stop the modem RPC daemon and the mux
# reporter. Neither needs a ctl. write today -- vendor.ccci_rpcd is not
# `disabled` so init auto-starts it, and muxreport is restarted by init's own
# `start` builtin from `on property:vendor.ril.muxreport=1` -- so no domain is
# granted them. If a modem-reset restart path is ever needed, add exactly one
# set_prop() and say which binary carries the service-name literal.
type ctl_mtk_ccci_fsd_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_ccci_rpcd_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_gsm0710muxd_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtkrild_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_rilproxy_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_muxreport_prop, property_type;

type mtk_ril_mux_prop, property_type;
type mtk_ril_mux_report_prop, property_type;
type mtk_muxreport_state_prop, property_type;
type mtk_ril_active_md_prop, property_type;
type mtk_modem_status_prop, property_type;
type mtk_ccci_response_prop, property_type;
type mtk_vendor_radio_prop, property_type;

# Two more prefixes the modem stack writes that were landing on
# vendor_default_prop and being denied at every boot:
#   vendor.net.handover.thlte    from mtkmal   (LTE<->WiFi handover threshold)
#   vendor.cdma.ril.uicc.mccmnc  from mtkrild  (UICC operator, CDMA namespace)
# An allow rule on vendor_default_prop would in fact compile here --
# public/domain.te:535 is wrapped in compatible_property_only() and this
# product leaves PRODUCT_COMPATIBLE_PROPERTY false -- but it would hand these
# two daemons every unlabelled vendor.*/persist.vendor.*/ro.vendor.* property
# in the image to get two prefixes working. The device type is least privilege,
# and it is the same discipline that keeps unnamespaced properties out of
# default_prop, whose neverallow (domain.te:525) IS compiled and cannot be
# worked around. This is the pattern vendor/property.te documents in full.
type mtk_ril_handover_prop, property_type;
type mtk_ril_cdma_prop, property_type;
type mtk_telephony_sensitive_prop, property_type;
type mtk_radio_config_prop, property_type;
type mtk_ccci_config_prop, property_type;
type mtk_nvram_ready_prop, property_type;

# Voice IMS configuration is read by the phone-UID APK. Runtime service
# control remains split per daemon and writable only by the MAL coordinator.
type mtk_ims_prop, property_type;
type mtk_ims_feature_prop, property_type;
type mtk_volte_runtime_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_volte_imcb_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_volte_stack_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_volte_ua_prop, property_type;

# vendor.net.ims.* -- written by volte_stack, which links libipsec_ims_shr.so.
# Observed live on the first image where the IMS stack got far enough to
# publish anything:
#   avc: denied { set } for property=vendor.net.ims.volte.pid
#        scontext=u:r:volte_stack:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_default_prop:s0
# Unlabelled it falls to vendor_default_prop. That denial is real and was
# measured, but note it is a permission denial, not a neverallow: domain.te:535
# does not compile on this product (compatible_property_only(), see
# vendor/property.te), so the alternative to typing would have been granting
# volte_stack set on the whole vendor.* catch-all. Typed instead, on least
# privilege -- the same reason unnamespaced properties get types to stay clear
# of the default_prop neverallow at domain.te:525, which does compile.
# Stock labels the whole `vendor.net.` prefix mtk_network_prop and grants its
# rcs_volte_stack `set` on it; this tree scopes the type to the one subtree a
# shipped daemon actually writes. The other reader that will matter is
# ipsec_mon, which reads vendor.net.ims.ipsec.version -- see WI-018.
type mtk_ims_net_prop, property_type;

# ccci_mdinit is the only domain with write on mtk_para_block_device, the
# bootloader control block (clients.te). mal-daemon coordinates the three VoLTE
# daemons. See the header in nvram/vendor/property.te for why none of the
# ctl_* types in this tree has a setter.
#
# NOT labelled here: ctl.ccci3_mdinit. That service no longer exists -- MD3 is
# retired with the C2K stack -- so the name resolves to nothing whatever its
# label.
#
# Also not labelled, deliberately: the three services whose real control channel
# is a radio property rather than ctl.  mal-daemon is restarted by init from
# `on property:vendor.ril.mal`, vendor.ril-daemon-mtk from vendor.ril.mtk and
# vendor.ril-proxy from vendor.ril.rilproxy; all three properties are
# mtk_vendor_radio_prop, which mtkmal, rild, mtkrild, gsm0710muxd and
# vendor_init can set. Labelling ctl. shuts one door of two. It is still worth
# shutting -- system_app can reach ctl_default_prop and none of those five --
# but do not record it as full containment.
type ctl_mtk_ccci_mdinit_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_mal_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_imsa_prop, property_type;
type ctl_mtk_wfo_prop, property_type;
